# Things a child can and cannot do, said, asked and answered.
lesson language-i-can v=1 format=teach grade=5 unit=3 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "What I can do"
  goal "Say what you can and cannot do in [[language.name]], and ask someone what they can do."

  look {
    scene 20x8 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        row moves space=2 align=end {
          person a pose=run age=child hair=ponytail top=mint
          person b pose=jump age=child hair=short top=tang
          person c pose=balance age=child hair=curly top=berry
        }
      }
    }
    say """
      To say what you can do, say [[ability.i-can-swim]], I can swim. To say what you cannot do, say
      [[ability.i-cannot]]: [[ability.i-cannot-cook]], I cannot cook. To ask, say
      [[ability.can-you-swim]], can you swim? Ana runs, Leo jumps and Mia balances: each of them can
      do something.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.i-can-name k=4
    }
    show language.i-can-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.i-can-meaning k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show language.i-can-write k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.i-can-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=6
    show language.i-can-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.i-can-write k=4 easy-k=2 hard-k=6
    show language.i-can-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.i-can-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.i-can-write k=4
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show language.i-can-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.i-can-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[ability.i-can-swim]] I can swim. [[ability.i-cannot-swim]] I cannot swim. [[ability.can-you-swim]] Can you swim?"
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is can and cannot taken for each other because the sentences are the
    same but for one small word: every naming question offers the sentence with that word added or
    left out, and one with another thing done. In [[language.name]], being able to do something that
    has been learnt, such as swimming, is said with [[ability.i-can]], I know how to, which is why
    the lesson uses it. The step up from my week of this grade is from what the child does to what
    the child is able to do, said, asked and answered. From this grade the written words are marked
    with their accents: a word typed without its accent is not taken. The dictation is read from
    this sheet, where it prints as Read aloud, or played by the device. If the child is stuck, act
    each thing out and say it with yes or no. Off the page, make a family poster of what each person
    can do, in the language. It meets England's key stage 2 languages programme (years 3 to 6,
    statutory from age 7): "speak in sentences, using familiar vocabulary, phrases and basic
    language structures"; Japan's grades 5 and 6 foreign languages: "Can communicate in asking and
    answering questions about themselves, someone they communicate with and their surroundings by
    using simple words and phrases and basic expressions on the spot" (Japan's grade five book We
    Can! 1 has a unit on I can, recalled); and CEFR A2, "Can communicate in simple and routine tasks
    requiring a simple and direct exchange of information on familiar and routine matters". The
    phrases are in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[ability.i-can-play-the-piano]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[ability.i-can-swim]] and [[ability.i-cannot-skate]] and the words to write are [[ability.to-sing]] and [[ability.to-dance]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[ability.i-can-play-chess]] and [[ability.i-can-jump-very-high]] and the words to write are [[ability.to-skate]], [[ability.to-run]] and [[ability.to-jump]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
